Hi John,

Not sure if this is relevant anymore in your studies (apologies for the delayed reply), but yes I would go with the multipleGroup() approach to include different properties of the populations, and fit a T dimensional MIRT model (one factor per time point) to compare changes across the pre-post tests. There are other considerations worth modeling here as well, such as allowing for changes in the hyper parameters in the post test scenario to track changes in the latent trait distributions, which will generally require additional constraints (e.g., fixing the item parameters in item 1 at time 1 to be equal to the item parameters in item 1 at time point 2). Depending on how much data you have available such models are useful, but their specification can be a little clunky syntactically speaking.

The socio demographic information to include is a little tricker as to me these come off as latent regression type effects w.r.t. to the latent trait decompositions, though perhaps you intended to treat these as grouping variables as well. If the latter you can create a new factor variable like group <- factor(Group):factor(Socdem1) to interact the variables and treat these as the input grouping IV instead, and follow up with wald() or likelihood ratio tests to further understand the nature of the effects. HTH, and again apologies for the (now potentially useless) reply....

Phil

